.TH ZM-API-TAKEOVER 8 "2026-10-02" "zm-api" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
zm-api\-takeover \- switch zm-api between passive and active daemon control
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zm-api-takeover
.RI [ \-\-yes ]
.RI [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B zm-api-takeover \-\-check
.br
.B zm-api-takeover \-\-revert
.RI [ \-\-yes ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Moves a ZoneMinder host between two supervision arrangements, sequencing both
services so they are never both in charge.
.PP
After installation
.BR zm-api (8)
is
.BR passive :
it serves the REST API and leaves ZoneMinder's daemons alone, so it coexists
with a running
.BR zoneminder.service .
That is the on-ramp rather than the destination \(em passive exists so the
switch happens when the operator chooses, not at install time.
.PP
In
.B active
(takeover) mode zm-api supervises
.BR zmc ,
.BR zmfilter ,
and the rest itself, replacing both
.BR zmdc.pl (8)
and
.BR zmwatch.pl (8)
with one native supervisor: exponential restart backoff that resets once a
daemon stays up, a reconciliation loop keeping running daemons in step with the
.B Monitors
table, daemon control over the REST API, and supervision events in the journal.
The legacy
.I zmdc.sock
IPC shim stays bound, so tooling written against
.BR zmdc.pl
keeps working.
.PP
Exactly one supervisor may run: two would fight over the same processes and
shared memory segments, producing daemons that restart in a loop and events
that record erratically. This script therefore stops and disables
.B zoneminder.service
before it flips zm-api's mode, and reverses that order on the way back.
.PP
Concretely, taking over: run the pre-flight checks, disable and stop
.BR zoneminder.service ,
set
.B APP_DAEMON__ENABLED=true
in the environment file, restart
.BR zm-api.service ,
then confirm the result.
Reverting: set
.B APP_DAEMON__ENABLED=false
and restart zm-api first \(em releasing the daemons \(em then enable and start
.BR zoneminder.service .
.SS Checks before and after
Before anything is stopped, the script confirms that zm-api is running in
passive mode, that it runs as the account ZoneMinder's daemons run as
.RB ( ZM_WEB_USER
in
.IR /etc/zm/zm.conf ),
that this account can read
.I zm.conf
and write the events, socket, log and shared-memory directories, and that it
can execute
.BR zmc .
A failed check stops the takeover with ZoneMinder untouched.
.PP
After the switch, the script waits for zm-api to stay up and, when
.B zmc
was capturing beforehand, for zm-api to have started it again. If that does
not happen within the timeout the switch is undone:
.B APP_DAEMON__ENABLED
goes back to false and
.B zoneminder.service
is enabled and started. A takeover that cannot record does not leave the host
recording nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Run the pre-flight checks and exit. Changes nothing. Exit status 0 when the
host is ready, 1 when it is not.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Take over even when a pre-flight check fails. The check after the switch, and
the rollback it triggers, still apply.
.TP
.BR \-\-revert ", " \-\-passive
Hand daemon control back to ZoneMinder.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Skip the confirmation prompt. Intended for automation.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH PREREQUISITES
.IP \(bu 2
Run as root.
.IP \(bu 2
zm-api must already be working in passive mode. Takeover is not a way to fix a
broken install: if the API cannot reach the database, taking over ZoneMinder's
daemons as well means nothing records.
.IP \(bu 2
zm-api must run as the account ZoneMinder's daemons run as, so that recordings
keep one owner whichever supervisor started
.BR zmc .
The package arranges this at install time. If ZoneMinder was installed
afterwards, run
.I /usr/share/zm-api/setup-instance.sh
and restart zm-api.
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing else may be starting the ZoneMinder daemons \(em no cron entry, no
.BR zmpkg.pl
invocation, no second host sharing the database with the same server ID.
.SH VERIFYING
After takeover, confirm that the daemons are running and that zm-api owns
them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl is-active zm-api          # expect: active
systemctl is-enabled zoneminder     # expect: disabled (or "not-found")
ps -o ppid=,cmd= -C zmc             # expect: one zmc per enabled monitor,
                                    #         parented by the zm-api process
journalctl -u zm-api -n 50
.EE
.RE
.PP
Then confirm recording actually continues: watch the event count for an active
monitor rise over a few minutes. A clean
.B systemctl status
proves the supervisor started, not that video is being captured.
.SH REVERTING
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo zm-api-takeover --revert
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reverting is the first thing to try if capture misbehaves after a takeover: it
restores the arrangement the host had before, and the REST API keeps working
throughout because passive mode is its normal state. If the script cannot run,
the manual equivalent is to set
.B APP_DAEMON__ENABLED=false
in
.IR /etc/zm-api/zm-api.env ,
.BR "systemctl restart zm-api" ,
then
.BR "systemctl enable --now zoneminder" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ZM_API_ENV_FILE
Path to the environment file to edit. Default
.IR /etc/zm-api/zm-api.env .
.TP
.B ZM_API_TAKEOVER_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for zm-api to stay up, and for capture to resume, before a
takeover is rolled back. Default 60.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a user-declined confirmation.
.TP
.B 1
Not run as root, the environment file does not exist, or a pre-flight check
failed. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 2
Unrecognised argument.
.TP
.B 3
The takeover was attempted, failed its check, and was rolled back.
ZoneMinder is supervising its daemons again.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/zm-api/zm-api.env
Edited in place to set
.BR APP_DAEMON__ENABLED .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zm-api (8),
.BR zm-api.env (5),
.BR zmdc.pl (8),
.BR zmpkg.pl (8)
